WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:01.339
Hello there!

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:04.340
Look, Ms. Coffee Bean is asleep... [sleeping
sounds].

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.520
Oooh, isn’t that sweet?

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.520
Well, while she sleeps, I wanted to show you
some of my own work.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.220
Ms. Coffee Bean always pressures me to cover
the notorious papers, because those are the

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.670
safest bet that will interest YOU and get
HER the clicks... and the views!

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:28.050
Okay, so..., quickly, while she sleeps, I
would like to tell you about my own research

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.350
and how that is going.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:35.120
In a nutshell… you know about the cool idea
to replace the self-attention with the Fourier

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.120
Transform?

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.080
Yeah, it's not like that.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.900
But you know what, it is honest work and interesting,
nonetheless.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.590
But first, I had to find myself some collaborators.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.460
Or they found me, actually.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:50.460
You know what?

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.410
We found each other!

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:56.210
So this is what we were up to, me and my collaborators…

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:01.420
There was no Ms. Coffee Bean involved, so
better not wake her up, okay …?

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.959
Our work is entitled in short “Seeing past
words”; because we are investigating whether

00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:14.630
vision and language models really integrate
both the visual and the textual modalities,

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.420
as opposed to faking that they are multimodal.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:25.139
I mean that there is reason to believe, that
models meant for integrating image and text

00:01:25.139 --> 00:01:30.659
are actually focusing on language only, exploiting
biases in the textual input.

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:33.469
It was highlighted by previous work.

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:38.659
This question of whether models are mostly
exploiting unimodal signals, has become more

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:45.450
and more interesting since the dawn of transformer-based
vision and language models, which follow the

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.899
pre-train and fine-tune paradigm, such as
LXMERT, ViLBERT, and many others.

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:56.079
I’ve talked with Ms. Coffee Bean quite some
times about them on this channel.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:02.669
Quick reminder: their pre-training includes
multimodal masked language modelling, and

00:02:02.669 --> 00:02:07.739
image-sentence alignment, where the goal is
to predict whether an image and a caption

00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:10.519
match
or mismatch.

00:02:10.519 --> 00:02:17.930
This pre-training on large amounts of paired
image-text data, enables “cross-modal" vision

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:24.599
and language representations that seem "universal",
usable for any task.

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:31.599
Then taking such a pre-trained model and fine-tuning
on downstream tasks like these ones here,

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:37.459
is a good recipe for achieving at least CLOSE-TO
state-of-the-art performance.

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:43.290
So, given the success of these vision and
language models, it is important to ask:

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.000
How cross-modal are they really?

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.769
We choose to answer this research question
by developing a counting probe.

00:02:51.769 --> 00:02:54.400
Why counting?

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.120
Because counting nicely combines visual and
textual reasoning.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:05.060
Counting requires: the detection of object
instances in the image, mapping them to categories,

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.769
and also properly aligning the instances to
references in the textual input.

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:16.120
This makes counting a very cross-modal task,
thus should be solvable only by multimodal

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.120
models.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.150
[Ms. Coffee Bean sleeping noises]

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:25.959
We formulate the counting task in a so-called
FOIL setup, by having images with either correct

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:32.150
counting-related captions, or foiled captions,
which are slight, but incorrect modifications

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.319
of the original caption.

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:41.140
We generate these examples automatically,
based on the VQA dataset called Visual7W.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.409
We work with an interrogative format, but
also with a declarative one.

00:03:45.409 --> 00:03:51.469
The model’s task is to judge whether an
unambiguous question or statement about the

00:03:51.469 --> 00:03:57.969
number of entities visible in a natural image,
is correct, or a foil.

00:03:57.969 --> 00:04:03.920
We conduct experiments with ViLBERT, ViLBERT
12-in-1 and LXMERT in zero-shot and fine-tuned

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.110
settings.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:09.950
All three models were pre-trained with the
image-sentence alignment task, check this

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:13.659
slide and the paper for more details.

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:20.150
This image-sentence alignment is important
to us, giving us the opportunity to look at

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:25.550
the alignment score assigned by the model,
which reflects the predicted probability that

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.620
text and image match.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:33.500
We use the image-sentence alignment head during
zero-shot testing to tell which ones are correct

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:41.060
or incorrect counting examples and compute
an accuracy score on this binary classification

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.080
task.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.960
Results in zero-shot show that the models
cannot solve the counting task.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:47.960
[oh]

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:52.210
Prominently, ViLBERT has a preference to predict
the correct class, to the detriment of the

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.830
foil class.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:59.949
But we compute also a more permissive score,
the pairwise accuracy ranking,

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:04.650
where for a successful model response, it
is enough if the correct caption ranks higher

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.520
in image-sentence-alignment than the foiled
one.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.800
Here we see that ViLBERT 12-in-1 has a better
response.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:19.009
The result on the declarative format are only
marginally better.

00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:24.860
So the poor performance in the zero-shot setting
isn't due to the fact that models trained

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:29.970
on image-sentence alignment could have trouble
with the question-answer format.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:34.910
In fine-tuning the picture seems to change
to the better, with performances increasing

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.300
to even 81%!

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:41.940
[Ms. Coffee Bean]: But does this mean that
these models really count?

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:50.000
No, or so our next experiment on the interpolated
split says, where we finetune on even numbers

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.050
and test on odd numbers.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:57.581
If the models are actually counting, then
this should generalize sufficiently for them

00:05:57.581 --> 00:06:01.610
to solve the task in the interpolated case.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:09.300
But when we do this, Ms. Coffee Bean, the
model’s accuracy drops to random chance.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:15.250
And we have even more experiments, to underline
the results we got so far with the image-sentence-alignment

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:16.250
head.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:20.909
So, we have experiments with masked language
modelling, where we let the model replace

00:06:20.909 --> 00:06:21.909
the numeral.

00:06:21.909 --> 00:06:30.000
The overall accuracy looks quite good at 55%,
but it is just an artefact of the model predicting

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.880
the most frequent numerals in training, like
0, 1 and 2, but being unable to go beyond

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.740
2.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:45.770
The same picture is painted by letting the
model answer the question, in VQA.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:50.530
For all the details, and even more experiments
for this, make sure to check out the paper

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:53.910
linked in the description below!

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:59.680
Therefore, Ms.Coffee Bean...!

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:03.729
... by means of our counting probe, we have
found that vision and language models are

00:07:03.729 --> 00:07:09.900
still prone to biases, and seem to learn broken
heuristics that clearly does not generalize

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.900
well.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:15.180
For further investigation, we encourage the
use of our counting probe for diagnosing the

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.460
absence of proper cross-modal alignment.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.800
Check out the link to the data on GitHub in
the description below.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.039
Let us know what you think or want to ask
in the comments!

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:27.759
Ms. Coffee Bean, how long have you been awake
now?

00:07:27.759 --> 00:07:32.169
Oh, no, so heard the whole thing.

00:07:32.169 --> 00:07:35.810
Are you mad on me now?

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:40.050
It’s research on multimodality, it's your
favorite, right?

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.379
You are mad, right?

00:07:42.379 --> 00:07:43.539
Ah... bye!

00:07:43.539 --> 00:07:44.119
Bye!

